{"tool_name":"sirius","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"sirius","home":"http://bio.informatik.uni-jena.de/sirius","summary":"Tool for metabolite identification using mass spectrometry.","addition_date":"2017-01-17T14:50:43Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T13:47:45.111159Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Desktop application"],"collections":["Proteomics","ms-utils"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:sirius","credit":[{"email":"sebastian.boecker@uni-jena.de","name":"Sebastian Boecker","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Maintainer"]},{"email":"webmaster@ms-utils.org","typeEntity":"Person","typeRole":["Documentor"],"url":"http://ms-utils.org"}],"documentation":[{"type":["Training material"],"url":"https://bio.informatik.uni-jena.de/sirius-training/"}],"edit_permission":{"authors":["n.m.palmblad@lumc.nl","proteomics.bio.tools"],"type":"group"},"function":[{"input":[{"data":{"term":"Mass spectrometry data","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2536"},"format":[{"term":"MGF","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3651"},{"term":"mzML","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3244"},{"term":"mzXML","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3654"}]}],"note":"outputs mzTab-M","operation":[{"term":"Isotopic distributions calculation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3632"},{"term":"Mass spectrum visualisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3694"},{"term":"Natural product identification","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3803"}],"output":[{"data":{"term":"Chemical formula","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_0846"},"format":[{"term":"TSV","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3475"},{"term":"xlsx","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3620"}]},{"data":{"term":"Evidence","uri":"http://edamontology.org/data_2042"},"format":[{"term":"mzTab","uri":"http://edamontology.org/format_3681"}]}]}],"name":"Sirius","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"msutils_import","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btn603","metadata":{"abstract":"Motivation: High-resolution mass spectrometry (MS) is among the most widely used technologies in metabolomics. Metabolites participate in almost all cellular processes, but most metabolites still remain uncharacterized. Determination of the sum formula is a crucial step in the identification of an unknown metabolite, as it reduces its possible structures to a hopefully manageable set. Results: We present a method for determining the sum formula of a metabolite solely from its mass and the natural distribution of its isotopes. Our input is a measured isotope pattern from a high resolution mass spectrometer, and we want to find those molecules that best match this pattern. Our method is computationally efficient, and results on experimental data are very promising: for orthogonal time-of-flight mass spectrometry, we correctly identify sum formulas for >90% of the molecules, ranging in mass up to 1000 Da. \u00a9 2008 The Author(s).","authors":[{"name":"Bocker S."},{"name":"Letzel M.C."},{"name":"Liptak Z."},{"name":"Pervukhin A."}],"citationCount":250,"date":"2009-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"SIRIUS: Decomposing isotope patterns for metabolite identification"},"pmcid":"PMC2639009","pmid":"19015140","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Metabolomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3172"},{"term":"Proteomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0121"},{"term":"Proteomics experiment","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3520"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Sirius","home":"https://bio.tools/sirius","license":"GPL-2.0-only","version":"1.72.0","summary":"In high resolution mass spectrometry (HR-MS), the measured masses can be decomposed into potential element combinations (chemical sum formulas). Where additional mass/intensity information of respective isotopic peaks is available, decomposition can take this information into account to better rank the potential candidate sum formulas. To compare measured mass/intensity information with the theoretical distribution of candidate sum formulas, the latter needs to be calculated. This package implements fast algorithms to address both tasks, the calculation of isotopic distributions for arbitrary sum formulas (assuming a HR-MS resolution of roughly 30,000), and the ranked list of sum formulas fitting an observed peak or isotopic peak set.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:sirius/app/bio.informatik.uni-jena.de","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":false,"license":null,"public":true,"readme":false,"repository":{"anonymous":null,"online":null}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"training_material":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":140,"citations":[{"count":2,"year":"2011"},{"count":2,"year":"2015"},{"count":3,"year":"2010"},{"count":4,"year":"2012"},{"count":5,"year":"2020"},{"count":6,"year":"2013"},{"count":7,"year":"2016"},{"count":8,"year":"2017"},{"count":8,"year":"2018"},{"count":10,"year":"2022"},{"count":10,"year":"2026"},{"count":11,"year":"2014"},{"count":11,"year":"2025"},{"count":12,"year":"2023"},{"count":13,"year":"2019"},{"count":13,"year":"2024"},{"count":15,"year":"2021"}],"doi":"10.1093/bioinformatics/btn603","pmcid":"PMC2639009","pmid":"19015140","ref_count":12,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"-1"},{"count":1,"year":"1996"},{"count":1,"year":"2000"},{"count":1,"year":"2002"},{"count":1,"year":"2004"},{"count":1,"year":"2007"},{"count":1,"year":"2020"},{"count":2,"year":"2008"},{"count":3,"year":"2006"}],"title":"SIRIUS: decomposing isotope patterns for metabolite identification.","year":2008}]}],"readme":false,"summary":{"description":true},"website":{"bioschemas":false,"copyright"